# Content pages are real paths (/help, /glossary, …) served by one SPA bundle.
# Each exists as a pre-rendered file, as does every part page, so the catch-all
# at the bottom only sees unknown paths: it serves the app (which says "Page not
# found") with a 404 status, so a typo or dead link is not indexed as a copy of
# the home page. Never a redirect: that would send a crawler back to / and undo
# the point of having paths.
#
# Netlify matches these top to bottom and stops at the first hit, so the asset
# directories are listed first: without them a mistyped /models/foo.glb would
# return the HTML page with a 200 and the loader would try to parse it as GLTF.
/assets/*   /assets/:splat   404
/models/*   /models/:splat   404
/fonts/*    /fonts/:splat    404
/hdri/*     /hdri/:splat     404

# Each catalogue part is pre-rendered head-only to dist/parts/<id>.html
# (apply-prerender.mjs), so a part URL can carry its OWN canonical instead of the
# root's — without this rewrite the /* fallback below serves /index.html, whose
# hardcoded root canonical made all ~543 sitemapped part URLs point at / and
# cancel each other out. A 200 REWRITE (not a redirect) keeps the URL unslashed,
# matching the sitemap and each page's own canonical; a bare 301-to-slash would
# make the canonical point away from the URL it sits on. The file is FLAT on
# purpose: a directory index at parts/<id>/index.html makes Netlify 301 the
# unslashed URL to the slashed one before this rule is ever consulted (an
# existing path shadows a redirect), which is what the first version did.
# This is ABOVE /* so a
# part path never reaches the generic fallback. An id with no generated file
# (thermal paste, a typo) falls through to a 404 rather than a soft-200 — which
# is correct: it is not a page.
/parts/:id  /parts/:id.html  200

/*          /index.html      404
